{dt}=40y(370-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=40,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=370.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536868 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100943 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a4751337-93bf-5b47-8b3f-32100820b19a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Logistic Growth Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ba7e32a-043c-5931-9dab-fc2b20f12800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=3y+{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536305} {:answer/id #uuid "4c2e65ff-a8f3-5761-bf62-d366e3c46fca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=3-{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536408} {:answer/id #uuid "7567addf-e4b0-589e-a11d-3a3a13e6546a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=4-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536480} {:answer/id #uuid "c19039df-bdb3-5ee3-9b88-548c6a17e19b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=3+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536587} {:answer/id #uuid "f5f817ac-4342-536d-890b-3ceed2d07931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=3y-4{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5f817ac-4342-536d-890b-3ceed2d07931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=3y-4{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536678} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fb6b47e-6d7c-543e-a3d2-2ef7185b7ec9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aac8b725-2415-58f2-86b7-ef6ffd39cf4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-83853" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=3y-4{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=4y(\\frac{3}{4}-y).\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=4,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=\\frac{3}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100942 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a46e688b-117f-5394-bcbb-fbfb7a5b6c7b" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Logistic Growth Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bd23510-2db8-57da-b03b-19947512a51c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=1\\,000P(0.05+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536325} {:answer/id #uuid "2cd7eaeb-5a1a-5699-bd57-bb98c1bd9464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.05P(1\\,000-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536352} {:answer/id #uuid "3a51bae9-e725-5a29-b29e-cc1f2c4e37c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=1\\,000P(0.05-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536375} {:answer/id #uuid "e0fceb3e-eb15-5178-8697-6df9c91f70e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=-0.05P(1\\,000+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536631} {:answer/id #uuid "f2c768ba-28d5-5f1d-815a-361c780dab8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.05P(1\\,000+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cd7eaeb-5a1a-5699-bd57-bb98c1bd9464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.05P(1\\,000-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536352} :answer-field/id #uuid "0175e659-cba3-56a0-ab48-25d20caff549" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b4b9134-52af-5188-95f0-9a0da05afadf" :question/math-academy-id "q-83861" :question/problem "The growth rate of the crab population $P(t)$ in a certain lake is jointly proportional to the crab population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the lake's carrying capacity and the crab population. If the constant of proportionality is $0.05$ and the maximum possible crab population is $1\\,000,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the crab population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the crab population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the crab population, the crab population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.05,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=1\\,000.$ Therefore, the crab population is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.05P(1\\,000-P).\n$$"}]}
